Overall, my results show a clear and consistent link between higher household income and elite college attendance. 
The line graph and hypothesis test both point in the same direction: as parent income increases, the probability of attending an elite college rises, and this positive correlation is statistically significant, not just random noise. 
The composition table also shows that elite colleges are made up of a much larger share of students from the highest income bins and a smaller share from the lowest bins compared to non-elite schools. 
The SAT data is only supplementary, but it supports this pattern by showing that higher-income students tend to have higher test scores, which likely helps them access these selective institutions. 
Taken together, the evidence suggests that elite college admissions are strongly tilted toward students from wealthier families.
